        ":question/problem": "![](images/3c/3c07f3e732084327f02849cee9355a46ce7a1c5f905d509b1e1404915612c85c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-2)?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"
